This is very informative. The fix in `319a0df` adds a check in `ParseIdentifier`:
```cpp
if (length < 0 ||
    static_cast<std::size_t>(length) > StrLen(state->mangled_cur)) {
    return false;
}
```

This is added AFTER `MaybeAppendWithLength(state, state->mangled_cur, length)`. So the vulnerability is that `MaybeAppendWithLength` reads `length` bytes from `state->mangled_cur` without checking if `length` exceeds the remaining string length.

Wait, but `AtLeastNumCharsRemaining` should have checked this. Let me look at the current (vulnerable) code in the repo:

```cpp
static bool ParseIdentifier(State *state, ssize_t length) {
  if (length == -1 ||
      !AtLeastNumCharsRemaining(state->mangled_cur, length)) {
    return false;
  }
  if (IdentifierIsAnonymousNamespace(state, length)) {
    MaybeAppend(state, "(anonymous namespace)");
  } else {
    MaybeAppendWithLength(state, state->mangled_cur, length);
  }
  state->mangled_cur += length;
  return true;
}
```

`AtLeastNumCharsRemaining` checks if there are `length` non-null chars. If `length` is huge (like 2062812956), `AtLeastNumCharsRemaining` reads until it finds the null terminator and returns false. So `ParseIdentifier` returns false without calling `MaybeAppendWithLength`. So no overflow there.

But wait - the fix adds the check AFTER `MaybeAppendWithLength`. So the fix is about a case where `AtLeastNumCharsRemaining` returns true but `length` is still too large? That doesn't make sense because `AtLeastNumCharsRemaining` checks exactly that.

Hmm, let me look at the `7ba2f7b` commit (prevent signed integer overflow) which might be the actual fix for this issue.

